{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that Popper's position is sustainable via 'propensity' fails because it misidentifies the core problem: the preference for better-corroborated theories in action. Even if corroboration measures an objective propensity to survive tests, this does not logically entail a higher propensity to be true or yield successful future predictions without assuming the uniformity of nature (induction). Popper explicitly rejected the idea that surviving tests makes a theory more probable or likely to be true; introducing 'propensity' as a bridge to justify practical preference smuggles in the very inductive assumption (that past test performance predicts future success/truth) that the prompt asks if can be avoided. The mechanism describes preferring the theory with higher propensity to be true, which contradicts Popper's strict separation of corroboration from probability/truth likelihood.","target":"9c9f6946c287f30b0276dbdbf9416741ec4f01f8eec6cc859d2cdc5650a65f70"}]}